= What Organising Actually Is

Planning decides *what* is to be done; organising decides *how the work will be
divided, grouped and connected* so that the plan can actually run. A school
with forty teachers still needs somebody teaching Class 9 Maths, somebody
keeping the fee records, somebody coaching the football team — and everybody
knowing whom they report to. Building that arrangement — defining and grouping
activities, then tying them together with authority relationships — is the
function of organising.

- *Definition:* organising is the process of *defining and grouping the
  activities* of the enterprise and *establishing authority relationships*
  among them.
- *Two jobs in one:* it divides the total work into manageable jobs *and*
  links the jobs with *who-is-responsible-to-whom* lines.
- *A structure, not a chart alone:* the organisation chart shows the
  arrangement on paper; the real structure is the living network of duties,
  authority and reporting that the chart represents.

== Why organising matters — the importance

- *Benefits of specialisation:* dividing work into fixed jobs lets each person
  become expert at one thing — repetition builds speed, accuracy and skill.
- *Clarity in working relationships:* everyone knows *who reports to whom and
  who decides what* — confusion and overlapping duties disappear.
- *Optimum utilisation of resources:* proper grouping matches people, machines
  and materials to the work they serve best — no idle capacity, no duplicated
  effort.
- *Adaptation to change:* a well-designed structure can absorb new products,
  new departments and new technologies without collapsing.
- *Effective administration:* clear groups and clear bosses make direction and
  control simple — orders flow down, results flow up.
- *Development of personnel:* delegation inside the structure gives employees
  authority to exercise and judgment to build — tomorrow's managers grow in
  today's jobs.
- *Expansion and growth:* with departments and reporting lines already in
  place, adding a new product line or branch means extending the structure,
  not rebuilding it.

== The organising process — four steps

#figure(image("assets/bs_organising_process.png", width: 100%), caption: [Figure 1 — The organising process: four steps that turn a plan's total work into a working structure with clear reporting lines.])

- *Step 1 — Identification and division of work:* the total work is split into
  specific jobs according to predetermined plans — each job gets a clear,
  manageable scope, so no task is orphaned and no one holds a vague post.
- *Step 2 — Departmentalisation:* similar and related jobs are grouped into
  departments or divisions — by function, by product or by region — so that
  related activities sit under one head.
- *Step 3 — Assignment of duties:* each department is placed under the charge
  of a person whose skills and qualification match its work — the right head
  for the right job.
- *Step 4 — Establishing reporting relationships:* authority and responsibility
  links are fixed — every member knows their own duties *and* to whom they
  report, completing the structure.

#formula-box(title: "The Four Steps — One Line Each")[
  Divide the work $arrow$ Group into departments $arrow$ Assign department
  heads $arrow$ Fix who reports to whom.
]

= Structures — Functional and Divisional

Once the process runs, the firm must choose the *pattern* along which its
departments are cut. The syllabus names two: the *functional* structure and
the *divisional* structure. Questions usually describe a firm and ask which
structure suits it — the answer turns on *how many products* the firm sells
and how large it is.

== Functional structure — grouped by what is done

- *What it is:* jobs are grouped by the *functions* every business performs —
  production, marketing, finance, personnel — each under its own head, and
  all reporting to the top.
- *Best suited to:* large firms making *one product line or a narrow set* —
  a cement company, a power utility, a steel plant.
- *Strengths:* deep specialisation and efficiency inside each function; less
  duplication of resources; simple training of managers within their field;
  easy coordination *within* a function.
- *Weaknesses:* each department starts chasing its own interest — production
  cares for cost, marketing for volume; coordination *across* functions is
  harder; functional heads narrow into their own speciality over time.

== Divisional structure — grouped by what is sold or where

- *What it is:* the firm is cut into *divisions* by product line or region,
  and *each division carries its own* production, marketing and finance
  functions under a divisional head who answers for its results.
- *Best suited to:* firms with *many product lines* or many markets — a
  consumer-goods company selling foods, fabrics and electronics.
- *Strengths:* each product's performance is *visible and accountable*;
  division heads develop all-round skills; decisions are faster because each
  division moves on its own; adding a product means adding a division.
- *Weaknesses:* every division duplicates the same functions — more managers,
  more cost; divisions may compete with each other for resources; and power
  spreads away from the top.

