= Formal and Informal Organisation

Inside every enterprise two organisations coexist: the one drawn on the chart
and the one grown by friendship. Both are real, both matter, and questions
keep testing whether you can tell them apart and weigh each one's strengths.

== Formal organisation — the designed structure

- *Meaning:* the *deliberately created* structure of authority and
  responsibility relationships, drawn to achieve organisational goals and
  shown in the organisation chart.
- *Features:* defined authority and responsibility at every position;
  communication follows official channels; it is impersonal — duties attach to
  the *post*, not the person.
- *Strengths:* accountability is fixed — performance can be pinned on
  positions; no overlapping of duties; stability survives personnel changes.
- *Weaknesses:* it can be rigid and slow; official channels delay messages;
  and it treats people as posts, ignoring their social needs.

== Informal organisation — the grown network

- *Meaning:* the network of *personal and social relationships* that arises
  spontaneously when people interact at work — friends who lunch together,
  colleagues who help each other without being asked.
- *Features:* no rules or chart — it *grows from within*; leaders emerge by
  acceptance, not appointment; communication runs through the *grapevine*,
  fast and unofficial; it satisfies *social* needs — belonging, friendship,
  recognition.
- *Strengths:* information and help travel fast; members feel supported and
  morale rises; it fills gaps where the formal structure is silent.
- *Weaknesses:* the grapevine spreads *rumours* as happily as facts; it can
  resist change the firm needs; and nobody is *accountable* inside it.

