  [Stability], [Stable; survives people changes], [Unstable; changes with feelings and members],
)

- *The balanced view:* the informal organisation is neither enemy nor
  ornament — managers who *listen* to it gain a fast early-warning system,
  and managers who fight it gain resistance.

= Delegation — Sharing Authority Downward

No manager can personally make every decision and perform every task — a
sales manager handling fifty customers cannot also draft every invoice. So
the manager *delegates*: passes part of the work to subordinates, grants them
the authority needed to do it, and remains answerable for the result.

- *Meaning:* delegation is the transfer of *authority* from a superior to a
  subordinate to perform *assigned duties*, while the superior retains
  answerability for the outcome.
- *Delegation never ends responsibility:* passing the duty down does not
  lighten the boss's answerability — if the subordinate fails, the boss
  answers for it.

== The three elements of delegation

- *Authority — the right to decide and act:* the granted power to use
  resources, make decisions and give orders within limits. Authority flows
  *downward* from superior to subordinate.
- *Responsibility — the duty to perform:* the obligation to carry out the
  assigned task properly. Responsibility flows *upward* — the subordinate
  owes performance to the boss.
- *Accountability — the answerability for results:* the obligation to answer
  for how the authority was used and the duty performed. Accountability
  *cannot be delegated or shared* — it always rests with the original
  superior and flows only *upward*.

#formula-box(title: "The Three Elements — The Balance Rule")[
  - *Authority* = the right to decide; *Responsibility* = the duty to perform;
    *Accountability* = the answerability for the outcome.
  - Authority must *match* responsibility: authority without responsibility
    corrupts; responsibility without authority frustrates.
  - Accountability can *never* be delegated — the boss stays answerable.
]

== Why delegation matters

- *Reduces the workload of managers:* routine tasks move down, freeing the
  manager for planning, policy and exceptions.
- *Develops employees:* exercising real authority builds skill, confidence
  and judgment — the firm grows its own future managers.
- *Enables quick decisions:* decisions are taken where the information is —
  no climbing the hierarchy for every small matter.
- *Basis of the hierarchy:* every level of the organisation chart exists
  because some authority was delegated to it.
- *Supports expansion and growth:* a firm can only grow as wide as its
  managers can delegate — delegation multiplies one manager's capacity.

= Decentralisation — Authority Spread Across Levels

Delegation passes authority down in *one relationship*; decentralisation is
the *policy* of dispersing decision-making authority *across the whole
organisation*. The top management deliberately keeps the least necessary
power and pushes the rest down to every level — divisions and departments
decide their own operations without running upstairs for each one.

- *Meaning:* decentralisation is the *systematic dispersal of authority* to
  the lowest levels at which it can be exercised competently — the top's
  conscious choice to delegate widely.
- *It is a policy, not an act:* delegation is an unavoidable feature of any
  hierarchy; decentralisation is *optional* — the top chooses how much to
  disperse and at what pace.

== Why decentralisation matters

- *Develops initiative and confidence:* when all levels exercise real
  authority, employees learn judgment — the organisation's decision-making
  muscle grows at every joint.
- *Develops future managers:* handling real division-level decisions trains
  a whole bench of managers — promotion becomes a step, not a leap.
- *Quick decisions at the spot:* people closest to the problem decide it, so
  customers and machines do not wait for headquarters.
- *Relieves the top management:* routine and operational decisions drain away
  from the top, which then concentrates on strategy and policy.
- *Better control:* divisions become measurable units — their results can be
  compared, ranked and corrected (the profit-centre idea).
- *Supports growth:* a decentralised firm can add products and branches
  without clogging the top's desk.

== Delegation vs decentralisation — the classic comparison

#table(
  columns: (1.1fr, 1.5fr, 1.5fr),
  table.header[*Point*][*Delegation*][*Decentralisation*],
  [Meaning], [A superior grants authority to *a subordinate* for assigned duties], [*Systematic dispersal* of authority to all levels of management],
  [Origin], [Arises from the *need* — every hierarchy delegates; compulsory by nature], [Top management's *voluntary policy choice*],
  [Scope], [Between *two individuals* — one boss, one subordinate], [*Organisation-wide* — spreads across levels and divisions],
  [Control], [Control stays with the delegating superior], [Control shifts to *results* — divisions judged by outcomes],
  [Freedom of action], [Limited freedom for the subordinate], [Maximum freedom at every level],
  [Status], [An act, a *process*], [The *result* of policy — extended delegation],
)

#analogy-box(title: "Everyday Example — The Hostel Warden")[
  A head warden *delegates* when she asks one assistant to collect the
  attendance sheets — one task, one grant of authority, and she still answers
  to the principal for the hostel. The hostel is *decentralised* when the
  principal lets each warden run mess menus, discipline and budgets for their
  own block without asking — authority spread as policy across all blocks,
  judged by results. One act, one relationship — versus a philosophy for the
  whole institution.
]

