= Common Errors and Special Cases

#exception-box(title: "Common Errors & Special Cases")[
  #table(
    columns: (1.35fr, 1.35fr, 1.2fr),
    table.header[*The mistake students make*][*What is right*][*The why / the trap*],
    ["Accountability can be delegated along with the task"], [Accountability *cannot* be delegated — it stays with the superior], [Duties move down, answerability does not; the boss still answers for the outcome.],
    ["Authority and responsibility can be unequal"], [They must *match* — extra authority corrupts, extra responsibility frustrates], [A subordinate with duty but no power blames the system; one with power but no duty misuses it.],
    ["Delegation and decentralisation are the same thing"], [Delegation = one boss to one subordinate; decentralisation = *organisation-wide dispersal policy*], [Scope and origin differ — compulsory act vs voluntary policy.],
    ["Decentralisation happens automatically in every organisation"], [It is *optional* — the top chooses how much authority to disperse], [Every hierarchy delegates something; not every firm decentralises.],
    ["The informal organisation harms the firm"], [It *helps and hurts* — fast communication and support, but rumours and change-resistance], [Smart managers use the grapevine as an early-warning system instead of fighting it.],
    ["Functional structure suits every big firm"], [It suits *single-product-line* firms; multi-product firms need divisions], [A foods-plus-electronics firm needs product accountability — that is the divisional logic.],
  )
]

- *Spot the pattern:* the unit's traps are *direction and scope* errors —
  which way authority flows, what cannot move, and how wide the power spread
  is. Always ask: one relationship or the whole organisation?
- *One special case:* if a question says "even after giving the work to his
  junior, the manager is answerable for the failure" — the tested fact is
  *accountability cannot be delegated*.

